<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create descriptive captions for images with simple words. Interactive: Edit a co-constructed text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create captions for images of bedtime routines (e.g., "Time to sleep."). Can edit and improve a co-constructed text about bedtime routines (e.g., revising "I brush my teeth." to "First, I brush my teeth. Then, I put on my pajamas. Last, I go to bed."). Can write short phrases and sentences on bedtime routines collaboratively (e.g., "I am sleepy.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying flashcards or large images of common evening routine activities (e.g., brushing teeth, wearing pajamas, reading a book, going to bed). Students chorally identify the actions using previously learned vocabulary: "brush teeth," "wear pajamas," "read book," "go to bed." The teacher asks, "What do you do before bed?" and encourages students to say one action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simple sentences about their own evening routine, using sequencing words and writing them on the board. For example, "First, I wash my face. Then, I brush my teeth. Last, I go to bed." The teacher points to each word as they say it, emphasizing the target pronunciation of /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth," and blending /w-ɒ-ʃ/ for "wash."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I brush my teeth *after* I go to bed? (No!) When do I brush my teeth?" (Before bed!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'First' mean?" (The beginning!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Evening Routine".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large poster or a projected slide show with 3-4 sequential images depicting a simple evening routine of a child. Each image has a short, clear caption written below it using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., Image 1: Child brushing teeth. Caption: "First, I brush my teeth." Image 2: Child wearing pajamas. Caption: "Then, I wear my pajamas." Image 3: Child reading a book in bed. Caption: "Next, I read a book." Image 4: Child sleeping. Caption: "Last, I go to bed. I am sleepy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Express simple ideas in writing related to daily routines, using basic sentence structures and sequencing words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist question: "What is this story about?" (A bedtime routine!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matching: The teacher gives students small cards with phrases like "brush teeth" or "read book." Students match the cards to the corresponding image on the poster or screen.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-9 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Sequencing words ("First, then, next, last"), Present simple for routines ("I brush my teeth," "I wear my pajamas").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: bed, pajamas, teeth, toothbrush, story, book, brush, wash, wear, read, sleepy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pragmatic: Describing evening routines.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with 3-4 pictures of bedtime routines. Below each picture, there is a sentence with a missing verb or noun. Students choose from a word bank (e.g., brush, wash, wear, read, teeth, pajamas, book) to complete the sentences. Example: "I ____ my ____." (picture of brushing teeth).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sequencing & Sentence Building:</b> The teacher projects 3-4 new images of evening routines in a mixed order. Students work in pairs to write simple sentences for each image, using sequencing words (First, Then, Next, Last) to put them in order. The teacher provides sentence frames on the board: "_____, I _______." (e.g., "First, I brush my teeth."). Teacher circulates to monitor and assist with vocabulary and sentence structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Check & Correction:</b> Students exchange their written sequenced sentences with another pair. They check each other's work for correct sequencing words, appropriate vocabulary, and basic sentence structure (e.g., subject-verb agreement for "I"). They use a simple checklist: "Are sequencing words used?", "Are the words correct?".</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Evening Routine".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Caption Creation and Editing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides each student (or small groups of 2-3) with a worksheet containing four blank picture frames. Students draw their own simple evening routine activities in each frame (e.g., washing face, putting on pajamas, going to bed, sleeping). Below each frame, they write a simple descriptive caption for their drawing. They must use at least three sequencing words (First, Then, Last) and incorporate vocabulary like "pajamas," "teeth," "bed," "sleepy," "wash," "brush." Students are encouraged to use time and status expressions like "It's bedtime!" or "I am sleepy." in their last caption.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After writing their initial captions, students exchange their worksheets with a partner. The partners then act as editors, reading the co-constructed captions and suggesting improvements using a simple "check" or "star" for good sentences and a question mark for sentences that need revision (e.g., "First, I brush my teeth. Then, I put on my pajamas. Last, I go to bed."). They focus on adding more details or using sequencing words correctly, as practiced. The teacher prompts with questions like, "Can you add 'Next'?" or "Is there a word for 'tired'?" (sleepy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Creating original written content, applying learned grammar and vocabulary, and engaging in interactive editing to improve clarity and sequence in describing their "My Evening Routine."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the editing process, and review of final written captions. A simple checklist can be used by the teacher (or for peer assessment). Students exchange their revised works for final peer checking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each pair (or individual, if preferred) holds up their completed and edited worksheet with drawings and captions. Some students may volunteer to read their sequence of captions aloud to the class, demonstrating their ability to describe their evening routine.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist/Peer Feedback):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least three sequencing words (First, Then, Last).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Included at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., brush, teeth, pajamas, bed, sleepy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Captions clearly describe the images.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in editing and improving text (for co-constructed texts).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about my evening routine: Yes / A little / No."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My favorite word from today is _________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you use in your writing today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult when writing your captions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The collaborative caption creation and peer editing seemed to work well, fostering both individual production and interactive refinement of text. Students successfully used sequencing words and most target vocabulary, indicating solid retention from previous lessons. Some students still struggle with basic sentence structure (e.g., missing "I" before verbs), which will require further scaffolding with sentence frames or guided practice in future lessons. The visual support was crucial for engagement and comprehension. Adjustments for next time: Ensure clear models for simple drawing, perhaps provide more sentence starters for lower-level writers, and dedicate a bit more time to the peer editing phase to allow for deeper interaction. Need to explicitly remind students about writing "Goodnight" in their captions or as a closing phrase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson on writing simple descriptions of evening routines will directly feed into Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration. Students will use their written routines to create a small "bedtime routine book" or a poster, and explain their routine to a friend, integrating visual, oral, and written communication. They might also summarize a friend's routine, which will require understanding and re-explaining the written work produced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>